## Included software

- Update *Xyz* to [1.2.3].

- Update *Xyz* from 1.0.0 to [1.2.3].

- Update *Tor Browser* to [1.2.3](https://blog.torproject.org/new-release-tor-browser-123/).

<!--

- Update *Tor Browser* to [1.2.3](https://blog.torproject.org/new-release-tor-browser-123),
  which fixes [$CODE]($LINK), a (major|minor) $TYPE vulnerability. Using this
  vulnerability, an attacked could $IMPACT.

  <div class="attack">

  <p>We are (not) aware of this (attack|vulnerability) being (used|exploited) in practice.</p>

  </div>

  To give more details, use an inline that can be reused in /security like we
  did for TROVE-2023 in version_5.19.1.

-->

- Update the *Tor* client to 0.1.2.3.

- Replace *GNOME Videos* with *Celluloid*, a more modern and reliable video player.

  For more advanced features, you can install
  [[*VLC*|https://www.videolan.org/]] as [[additional
  software|doc/persistent_storage/additional_software]].

  [[!img celluloid.png class="screenshot" alt=""]]

- Remove *Thunderbird*.

  You can still [[install *Thunderbird* as additional
  software|doc/anonymous_internet/thunderbird]].

  If you have both the **Thunderbird Email Client** and **Additional Software**
  features of the Persistent Storage turned on, Tails automatically adds
  *Thunderbird* to your list of [[additional
  software|doc/persistent_storage/additional_software]].

  A new version of *Thunderbird* is released in Debian shortly after each Tails
  release, because both *Tails* and *Thunderbird* follow the [[release calendar
  of *Firefox*|https://whattrainisitnow.com/calendar/]]. As a consequence,
  until Tails 7.5 (February 2026), the version of *Thunderbird* in Tails was
  almost always outdated, with known security vulnerabilities.

  By installing *Thunderbird* as additional software, the latest version
  of *Thunderbird* is installed automatically from your Persistent Storage each
  time you start Tails.

- Stop making it possible to start our ISO images from a USB stick.

  Since [[2019|news/version_3.12]], we recommend *USB images* for most people
  to start Tails from a USB stick.

  We still distribute [[*ISO images*|install/download-iso]] to start Tails from
  a DVD or in a virtual machine. Until now, these ISO images worked on USB
  sticks as well, but provided a degraded experience without automatic upgrades
  or Persistent Storage.

  Our ISO images no longer work on USB sticks to save a few megabytes and
  prevent confusion for people using USB sticks.

- Strengthen the security confinement of *OnionShare* and *Unsafe Browser* by
  removing their access to the D-Bus session.

- Update (the *Linux* kernel to a.b.c|(some|most) firmware packages). <!-- Only if 'a.b' increased --> This improves support for newer
  hardware: graphics, Wi-Fi, and so on.

<!--

- Update the *Linux* kernel to a.bc, which fixes [DSA
  1234-1](https://lists.debian.org/debian-security-announce/2026/msg00035.html),
  multiple security vulnerabilities that could allow an application in Tails to
  gain administration privileges.

  For example, if an attacker was able to exploit other unknown security
  vulnerabilities in an application included in Tails, they might then use DSA
  6126-1 to take full control of your Tails and deanonymize you.

  <div class="attack">

  <p>This attack is very unlikely, but could be performed by a strong attacker,
